{{also|Bote|boté}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[bot#Old_English|bot]]
* [[boot#Etymology_2|boot]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|bote|bōte|advantage, benefit, profit; relief, salvation; atonement, amends, expiation; cure|lang=en}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|bot|bōt|help, relief, advantage, remedy; compensation for an injury or wrong; (peace) offering, recompense, amends, atonement, reformation, penance, repentance|lang=enm}}, from {{proto|Germanic|bōtō|recompense|lang=ang}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{term|bote|bōte|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|bota|bōta|lang=osx}}, Old High German {{term|buoza|lang=goh}}, Old Norse {{term|bot|bōt|lang=non}}, Dutch {{term|boete|lang=nl}}, German {{term|Buße|Buße|penance, atonement|lang=de}}, Swedish {{term|bot|lang=sv}}, Gothic {{term|𐌱𐍉𐍄𐌰||advantage|lang=got}} (bota).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/boʊt/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|pl=[[botes]] or [[boten]]}}

# The [[atonement]], [[compensation]], [[amends]], [[satisfaction]], [[expiation]]; as, [[manbote]], a compensation for a man slain.
#: ''Iesu ... For synne þat hath my soule bounde, Let þi blessed blood be my '''bote'''.'' &mdash; Iesu þat art heuene
# A [[payment]] of any kind
# A [[privilege]] or [[allowance]] of necessaries.
# {{legal|historic}} A right to take wood from property not one's own.
# [[repair]]s
#: ''Þey shulde..do '''bote''' to brugges þat to-broke were.'' &mdash; Pier's Plowman, 1400
# {{obsolete}} [[advantage]], [[benefit]], [[profit]], [[cure]], [[remedy]]
#: ''Heo lufeden bi wurten, bi moren, and bi rote; nas þer nan oðer '''boten'''.'' &dash; Layamon's Brut, 1275

====Usage notes====
* Often used to form compounds indicating a right to take wood only for a specific purpose.

